1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,755
Part four.

2
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:02,694
Round two.

3
00:00:03,053 --> 00:00:06,142
Kantrowitz put Marcus's biggest worries to Levie.

4
00:00:06,455 --> 00:00:07,303
The debt?

5
00:00:07,976 --> 00:00:10,530
Levie says everything depends on one thing:

6
00:00:10,797 --> 00:00:13,084
whether growth continues at some level.

7
00:00:13,444 --> 00:00:14,919
If the revenue comes,

8
00:00:15,023 --> 00:00:16,417
the commitments are fine.

9
00:00:16,776 --> 00:00:17,635
If it doesn't,

10
00:00:17,705 --> 00:00:18,843
they blow up the company.

11
00:00:19,748 --> 00:00:21,165
Token-maxing?

12
00:00:21,525 --> 00:00:23,742
He calls it a two-week fashion,

13
00:00:24,056 --> 00:00:27,365
not the reason Anthropic's revenue run rate passed

14
00:00:27,411 --> 00:00:28,491
$80 billion.

15
00:00:29,164 --> 00:00:30,430
Diminishing returns?

16
00:00:30,859 --> 00:00:33,460
He says improving a model for one field,

17
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:35,828
like cybersecurity or law,

18
00:00:36,026 --> 00:00:38,034
costs millions in training data,

19
00:00:38,173 --> 00:00:39,706
not hundreds of billions.

20
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:42,388
And each of those fields is worth trillions

21
00:00:42,423 --> 00:00:43,468
of dollars of work.

22
00:00:44,245 --> 00:00:46,358
Cheap Chinese and open models?

23
00:00:47,264 --> 00:00:49,122
Levie sees a rising tide.

24
00:00:49,389 --> 00:00:52,001
Companies use a top model to plan and

25
00:00:52,047 --> 00:00:53,707
cheap models for the bulk work,

26
00:00:54,021 --> 00:00:55,577
so they spend more on both.

27
00:00:56,355 --> 00:00:58,119
Marcus answers with a warning:

28
00:00:58,607 --> 00:01:00,221
more than a dozen studies,

29
00:01:00,244 --> 00:01:03,053
he says, show companies struggling to get a

30
00:01:03,135 --> 00:01:03,855
return,

31
00:01:04,284 --> 00:01:06,978
and hallucinations haven't gone away.

32
00:01:07,756 --> 00:01:09,172
Marcus gave ground too.

33
00:01:09,532 --> 00:01:11,471
He called his doubt about raising the money

34
00:01:11,552 --> 00:01:12,830
his weakest argument,

35
00:01:13,189 --> 00:01:16,278
as reports said the IPO was oversubscribed.

36
00:01:16,765 --> 00:01:19,378
He didn't dispute the tenfold revenue growth.

37
00:01:19,807 --> 00:01:22,141
And he agreed that the fallout from Jensen

38
00:01:22,210 --> 00:01:23,128
Huang's scandal

39
00:01:23,557 --> 00:01:26,111
could help Anthropic against Chinese rivals.

40
00:01:26,599 --> 00:01:28,805
"The best argument you've made," he told the

41
00:01:28,851 --> 00:01:29,350
host.

42
00:01:30,024 --> 00:01:31,011
And on one point,

43
00:01:31,208 --> 00:01:32,706
Levie conceded outright.

44
00:01:33,193 --> 00:01:35,353
Could the bills come due before the industries

45
00:01:35,399 --> 00:01:36,015
change?

46
00:01:36,688 --> 00:01:39,521
"Absolutely possible," he said.
